by how  we feel  our level of motivation our level of  interest and and even beyond that our  level of empathy with the language  and as one of the people who commented  on my blog said you know our desire or  willingness to see ourselves as part of  that group and i have always felt that  to be very significant  when i learn chinese i see myself as  chinese i have no trouble  seeing myself as one of them they may  not want me  or koreans or russians or whoever but i  don't care if i listen to ejamuskv and i  and i hear all these people i'd love to  go there and meet them and go out for  dinner and talk russian they may not  want anything to do with me  but but that's a different story in my  mind i can see myself as part of their  group  whether they would accept me as part of  the group in a way doesn't matter  and uh yeah i might face rejection that  might discourage me a little bit but in  a way it doesn't i just want to be part  of their group whether they want me or  not  and that kind of sort of emotional  commitment is very important  on the other hand  the idea that the language teacher has  to be the teacher of a new way of  thinking of a different culture has to  introduce the culture to it to you i  have always resisted that  if i was when i went to japan i actually  had for one month i went to  uh for an hour i think twice a week a  japanese class just under is underneath  tokyo tower  and the teacher there wanted to tell me  about japanese festivals and i can still  remember his kokuro kokoro and  all kinds of stuff like that about the  japanese and how they are  it was meaningless to me it didn't  i didn't want him  to  impose that on me i wanted to discover  it on my own  through the japanese people that i met  through the reading that i did  i didn't want to have it imposed on me  and so and maybe i'm  different or strange in that regard  but i always feel that any feelings that  we have that are natural  any desires that are genuine  are going to be much more powerful  motivators in learning  than something that is taught at us  instructed to us  so i picture these teachers  teaching and they don't really call it  critical think they call it higher order  thinking like they are going to teach  higher order thinking that suggests that  they are already practitioners of higher  order thinking and that their students  only practice lower order  thinking  i mean we all practice thinking  critical thinking i mean this thing cost  ten dollars and this costs five so if  this one isn't twice as good as that i  won't buy it i'll buy the cheaper one i  mean we're doing that all the time  um  but the people on this list are very  very insistent they're all without  exception and the other thing that's  funny on this list service because  everything that happens in the public  domain  insofar as language or any kind of  education is concerned it's  fundamentally driven by how do we get  more funding  and once funding stops the activity  stops  and this particular list serve is funded  and one of the things they fund  is a person to monitor every post  so that every so often i get one of my  posts because i send it an email there i  get it rejected  some while ago one of my posts was  rejected because i had the words in  there  you know sometimes it's useful to  consider both sides of an argument i  think i said something like that she  said please remove that  now here again  uh someone on this list serve  came along and said that the american  occupation of japan  after the war  is largely responsible for the japanese  approach to teamwork  and consensus building  so i went back and i said that  i did not think that a few years of  american occupation could have any such  influence  on japanese culture that was just not so  so then we had a back and forth and  someone else chimed in and he had a  bunch of  information about how  japan continues continues to operate on  a top-down military-style  you know management of management and  that the honcho is the man in charge and  everybody's afraid to challenge the  honcho and so i went back and said well  no actually there is no one there is no  such position as a honcho there's a  kacho there's a bucho there's no hancho  and  besides which in japan the the ideal is  consensus  uh  i think there's more military order  giving  type management in the u.s or canada  than there is in japan in my experience  and i lived there for nine years but you  also can have very dictatorial  management styles and different  companies different situations but by  and large the effort is to arrive at  some kind of consensus  and  but the japanese there is dissension  there is insubordination and i used as  an example the situation leading up to  the second world war  where the  sort of dissent  and the insubordination by a certain  group of hot-headed officers is largely  responsible for this sort of  increasingly aggressive posture  and aggressiveness and aggressive action  by the japanese military prior to the  war  and so i get a note back from the person  monitoring his farm  would you please remove your reference  to hot-headed officers because some of  the members of our group  may have connections with hot-headed  officers and may find that offensive  i kid you not that's what she said  so i i mean i've long since stopped i  don't argue with him i just but okay no  hard-headed officers just mid-level  officers whatever but that's just  unbelievable  you know  critical thinking but you can't  describe something that is true that is  historical fact  uh that probably is irrelevant to most  people on the farm anyway i'll leave the  subject  so i guess what i want to get at here  again is i don't want to belabor the  point that that  you know  the key thing for a teacher is to engage  the emotions  of the learner  and that is best done by somehow trying  to stimulate them so if if some  discussion critical discussion of i  think one of them said that they want  they want their learners to learn to be  able to maturely discuss ethical  questions well if the learner is  interested in discussing ethical  questions fine but if they want to talk  about cooking gossip  uh  travel whatever let them talk about what  they want  and let them find the things in your  culture that they find attractive don't  try to persuade them what they should  find  attractive because all  the main thing in teaching a language is  to turn on the switch  and that's what teachers should be  thinking about all the time how do i  turn on each person's switch now easier  said than done if you've got a room full  of apathetic teenagers but i'm about to  run out of time bye for now
 

